  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nVisible satellite shows hardly a cloud in the sky over the UP this \nafternoon, with high pressure centered over southern Quebec sending \nridging into the Great Lakes and water vapor revealing dry midlevel \nair over the region. Under sunny skies, temperatures are climbing a \ncouple degrees higher than yesterday, already in the lower to mid \n60s across much of the UP. Expect temperatures to peak in the mid to \nupper 60s for most this afternoon. Light winds and clear skies will \nallow temperatures to radiate out nicely again tonight, but we \nshould still come in a couple degrees warmer than in previous nights \nas a shift over to southerly flow brings in a comparatively warmer \nairmass. Lows range from the mid/upper 30s inland to the 40s near \nthe lakeshores. This should limit the extent of any frost to just \nthe coldest spots. Otherwise, model soundings show enough moisture \nat the lower levels to support at least some patchy fog overnight, \nbut this should not be particularly impactful. \n\nAnother quiet day is expected Thursday with ridging over the area, \nbut clouds increase during the afternoon and evening as a midlevel \nshortwave moves out of the Plains and heads towards the Great Lakes. \nThis could graze western Lake Superior and even the far western UP \nwith some showers Thursday evening through the first half of the \nnight, but there is a very dry airmass to contend with. Simulated \nreflectivity per the CAMs seems overly enthusiastic regarding the \nextent on any showers late Thursday, so will continue to lean \ntowards the NBM solution which keeps the UP dry. Similar to today, \ntemperatures should rebound into the mid 60s for most Thursday \nafternoon, but some spots may crack the 70 degree mark during the \nafternoon. Dewpoints look to mix out nicely once again, dropping \nback into the mid/upper 40s by the afternoon. This would yield \nminimum RH as low as the low/mid 40%s, lowest across the eastern UP \nwhere less cloud cover would support better mixing. Southerly winds \nremain tame, with gusts below 15mph, limiting the threat for fire \nspread.  \n\nSkies clear out Friday as ridging recovers in the wake of the \nexiting shortwave. High temperatures reach 5-10 degrees above \nnormal, peaking in the upper 60s to lower 70s. Split flow continues \nthrough Saturday as anomalously strong Canadian high pressure is \nmostly maintained, and the conjoining mid-level low deepens off the \nmid-Atlantic coastline. Saturday evening, a second shot of moisture \nrides the windward edge of a ridge centered over the Great Lakes \nnorth toward Saskatchewan. Chances for showers scrape the western UP \nlate Saturday into Sunday morning as another weak shortwave ripples \nthrough. Sometime Monday, high pressure at last appears to break \ndown over eastern Canada as a trough approaches from the North \nPacific. By midweek, ensembles and deterministic guidance bring this \nthrough the Great Lakes either as an open trough or a deep midlevel \nlow, with some phasing it with another more southerly feature. So, \nsignificant differences in the particulars as we enter the chaos \nregion of the guidance, but more widespread chances for rain finally \nreturn midweek next week.",
